<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!-- ONE entitlement, and it is the whole of what this process is allowed to do.

     `com.apple.security.application-groups = [group.com.relayium.app]` gives the
     extension a directory the containing app can also read. That is the entire
     hand-off: copy what the user shared into that directory, write a small plan
     describing it, and say on the sheet that the files are waiting in Relayium.
     Nothing is pushed to the app. A Share Extension may not open its containing
     app — Apple documents `NSExtensionContext.open(_:completionHandler:)` as the
     Today and iMessage extension points' method — so what brings the draft onto
     the Send tab is the user opening Relayium themselves.

     Everything else is absent, and each absence is load-bearing rather than
     merely unclaimed:

     No `keychain-access-groups`. The extension holds no bearer, reads no
     account, and generates no content key. The key for a shared draft is minted
     in the APP, at the moment the user presses Send, and filed under the pending
     job in the app's own default group. A keychain group here would be a
     credential share with a process that has nothing to share — and it would put
     the user's bearer one API call away from a target whose whole safety
     argument is that it cannot reach it.

     No `com.apple.developer.associated-domains`. The extension neither receives
     a link nor emits one. There is no `/share` hand-off URL, no custom scheme,
     no `NSExtensionContext.open` call and no responder-chain walk to
     `UIApplication` — `SharedDraftPreparationTests` scans this target's source
     for every one of those spellings. Claiming the domain would let this target
     be launched for a link it has no screen for.

     No `com.apple.developer.applesignin`. There is no sign-in here, and there is
     nothing to sign in FOR: staging files needs no account, which is why the
     Send tab shows a waiting draft to a signed-out user rather than the
     extension refusing to stage one.

     No network client, no background mode, no push. `IOSSurfaceGuardTests`
     checks the source of this target for `URLSession`, `URLRequest`,
     `AccountSession`, `bearerToken`, `TokenStore`, `Keychain`, `CloudUploader`
     and the rest, because "the extension makes no network request" is an
     absence, and an absence has no runtime to observe. -->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.relayium.app</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
